From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 21:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Six candidates for how a reflection reads

Patch 0021 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

The reflections came back and brought three complaints, each a different question, and the user's
instruction was "dont guess, add rungs to test". So each gets a candidate rather than an answer.

  31  the frame color is taken as the COMPOSITE will show it, not as the raster holds it
  32  the whole reflection is dimmed by the light falling on the water itself
  33  the frame is never used; the surface's own lit color stands in everywhere
  34  the ESTIMATE is never used. It is a lit vertex color times a constant standing in for a
      texture's average, so a BLACK surface comes back pale, which is the shape of "black turns
      white in reflections no matter the time of day" and needs no inversion to produce
  35  a point the frame cannot show takes the FOG's color rather than the nearest edge pixel.
      The clamp is what smears one pixel into "a stretching trail ... stretching endlessly"; the
      comment in that branch says the fog was tried and rejected for drawing a gray wedge, and
      that trade is now his to judge rather than mine to assume
  36  THE PRIMARY HIT ITSELF IS THE PLANE when it is water

36 is the one that stops the three wants pulling apart. Every rung before it had to choose between
water as a traced surface, so a fairy's glow can light it, and water NOT being the opaque hit, so
the probe can find something in front of it. They are the same question answered two ways. The way
out is to stop asking the probe for a surface in front: where the hit the frame already shows is a
horizontal face of a depthless translucent draw, it IS the water, it is lit like any other
surface, and the reflection has its plane without a second trace.

All six classify exactly as rung 30 does, so anything that changes is the candidate's doing.

With the ray tracing row Off none of this runs.
---
diff --git a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
--- a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
+++ b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
@@ -70,6 +70,9 @@ bool hitWritesDepth(int instanceId);
 // translucent, both reading depth, neither writing it, both plenty of triangles. They differ in
 // their NORMAL, which is not known when an instance is classified and is known here.
 bool hitIsWaterLike(uint instanceId, uint primitiveIndex);
+// The same question asked of the INSTANCE alone, for the raygen, which has a normal in the
+// payload but no primitive index to take one from.
+bool instanceIsDepthlessTranslucent(int instanceId);
 
 // IS A LADDER RUNG BELOW `rung` SELECTED (patch 0017)? The foliage work was settled on the
 // Experiment row and then made the DEFAULT, because a fix nobody can find is not shipped: that
@@ -318,6 +321,17 @@ void PrimaryRayGen() {
         if ((water.instanceId >= 0) && (water.hitNormal.y > 0.9f)) {
             gShadingSpecular[launchIndex] = float4(water.hitNormal, water.hitDistance);
         }
+        // F (rung 36): THE PRIMARY HIT ITSELF IS THE PLANE when it is water. Every rung
+        // before this one had to choose: water as a traced surface so a fairy can light it,
+        // or water NOT the opaque hit so this probe can find something in front of it. They
+        // are the same question answered two ways, and the way out is to stop asking the
+        // probe for a surface in FRONT. The payload already carries the normal and the
+        // distance of the hit the frame shows; where that hit is a horizontal face of a
+        // depthless translucent draw, it IS the water, and it is lit like any other surface.
+        if ((RtParams.tuneExperiment.x == 36.0f) && (payload.instanceId >= 0) &&
+            (payload.hitNormal.y > 0.9f) && instanceIsDepthlessTranslucent(payload.instanceId)) {
+            gShadingSpecular[launchIndex] = float4(payload.hitNormal, payload.hitDistance);
+        }
     }
     if (payload.instanceId >= 0) {
         // A hit is lit by the passes: the diffuse alpha is what the composite reads to apply the
@@ -715,11 +729,27 @@ float3 radianceFrom(float3 hitPosition, float3 shadedColor, bool hit, int pixelI
             const float frameLuma = dot(frame, float3(0.299f, 0.587f, 0.114f));
             const float estimateLuma = dot(estimate, float3(0.299f, 0.587f, 0.114f));
             if (frameLuma <= estimateLuma * OverlayRatio + 0.02f) {
-                return frame;
+                // A (31): this frame is the game's RASTER, before the composite multiplies
+                // it by the light, so a reflection of a dark scene comes back undarkened.
+                if (RtParams.tuneExperiment.x == 31.0f) {
+                    return frame * gDirectLightAccum[pixel].rgb;
+                }
+                // C (33): the frame is not used at all; the surface's own lit color stands
+                // in everywhere, which is what this already falls back to.
+                if (RtParams.tuneExperiment.x != 33.0f) {
+                    return frame;
+                }
             }
         }
     }
 
+    // D (34): the estimate is the surface's lit VERTEX color times a constant standing in
+    // for its texture's average, so for a BLACK surface it hands back a pale color. That is
+    // the shape of "black turns white in reflections no matter the time of day", and it
+    // needs no inversion to produce. Here the fog's color stands in instead.
+    if (RtParams.tuneExperiment.x == 34.0f) {
+        return fogColorOf(pixelInstance);
+    }
     return estimate;
 }
 
@@ -1152,7 +1182,16 @@ void ReflectionRayGen() {
             const float2 viewportOrigin = RtParams.viewport.xy;
             const float2 viewportSize = RtParams.viewport.zw;
             const float2 inViewport = clamp(float2(ndc.x * 0.5f + 0.5f, 0.5f - ndc.y * 0.5f) * viewportSize, 0.0f, viewportSize - 1.0f);
-            radiance = SrgbToLinear(gBackgroundColor.Load(int3(int2(viewportOrigin + inViewport), 0)).rgb);
+            // E (35): the clamp to the viewport's edge smears one pixel along the whole
+            // reflection, which is the stretching trail he sees. The comment above says the
+            // fog color was tried and rejected for drawing a gray wedge; both are now
+            // visible so the trade can be judged rather than assumed.
+            if (RtParams.tuneExperiment.x == 35.0f) {
+                radiance = fogColorOf(gInstanceId[launchIndex]);
+            }
+            else {
+                radiance = SrgbToLinear(gBackgroundColor.Load(int3(int2(viewportOrigin + inViewport), 0)).rgb);
+            }
         }
         else {
             radiance = fogColorOf(gInstanceId[launchIndex]);
@@ -1161,7 +1200,13 @@ void ReflectionRayGen() {
 
     const float cosTheta = saturate(dot(-direction, normal));
     const float fresnel = 0.02f + 0.98f * pow(1.0f - cosTheta, 5.0f);
-    gReflection[launchIndex] = float4(radiance * (fresnel * RtParams.tuneReflection.x), 1.0f);
+    // B (32): the whole reflection is dimmed by the light falling on the WATER itself, so a
+    // reflection lying in shadow is dark even when the thing it reflects is lit.
+    float3 reflectionOut = radiance * (fresnel * RtParams.tuneReflection.x);
+    if (RtParams.tuneExperiment.x == 32.0f) {
+        reflectionOut *= gDirectLightAccum[launchIndex].rgb;
+    }
+    gReflection[launchIndex] = float4(reflectionOut, 1.0f);
 }
 
 [shader("raygeneration")]
@@ -1387,6 +1432,16 @@ bool hitWritesDepth(int instanceId) {
     return true;
 }
 
+bool instanceIsDepthlessTranslucent(int instanceId) {
+    if (instanceId < 0) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    const RenderIndices ri = instanceRenderIndices[instanceId];
+    const RenderParams rp = DynamicRenderParams[ri.instanceIndex];
+    const OtherMode otherMode = { rp.omL, rp.omH };
+    return (otherMode.zMode() == ZMODE_XLU) && !otherMode.zUpd();
+}
+
 bool hitIsWaterLike(uint instanceId, uint primitiveIndex) {
     const RenderIndices ri = instanceRenderIndices[instanceId];
     const RenderParams rp = DynamicRenderParams[ri.instanceIndex];
@@ -1563,7 +1618,7 @@ void SurfaceAnyHit(inout SurfacePayload payload, in BuiltInTriangleIntersectionA
     // it was before the foliage work, so the primary reaches the bed, the shadow lands on the bed,
     // and the probe finds the water in front of it. A vertical one is foliage and is unaffected.
     if ((payload.coverage >= 0.0f) && hitIsWaterLike(InstanceID(), PrimitiveIndex()) &&
-        (RtParams.tuneExperiment.x == 30.0f)) {
+        (RtParams.tuneExperiment.x >= 30.0f) && (RtParams.tuneExperiment.x < 36.0f)) {
         IgnoreHit();
         return;
     }
@@ -1588,7 +1643,8 @@ void SurfaceMiss(inout SurfacePayload payload) {
 void ShadowAnyHit(inout ShadowPayload payload, in BuiltInTriangleIntersectionAttributes attributes) {
     // Water casts nothing either (patch 0019, rung 30), for the same reason and so that the two
     // rays agree about what water is.
-    if (hitIsWaterLike(InstanceID(), PrimitiveIndex()) && (RtParams.tuneExperiment.x == 30.0f)) {
+    if (hitIsWaterLike(InstanceID(), PrimitiveIndex()) && (RtParams.tuneExperiment.x >= 30.0f) &&
+        (RtParams.tuneExperiment.x < 36.0f)) {
         IgnoreHit();
         return;
     }
diff --git a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
--- a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
+++ b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
@@ -1932,6 +1932,20 @@ namespace RT64 {
